/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184219 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186490327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184219 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Rank or Nullity of a Matrix Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184219 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186184124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184219 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fcbc10c0-4e6b-5dac-ad87-f0175e4a53d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184421 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184421 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184421 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186184939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184421 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186185007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184421 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184421 #edb/ref 1045 "The Rank-Nullity Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184421 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186184937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184421 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184421 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184421 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "45b54b13-346d-538c-876e-285764d59813" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184754 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184754 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34833" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184754 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184754 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a $5\\times 8$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim(Null(}A))=3,$ then $\\text{rank}(A)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184754 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a05d1874-f484-5b03-a7ba-b2697b84a74b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184754 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =8 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =8-3 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184757 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184757 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34934" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184757 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $4 \\cdot 4$ matrix with $3$ pivot columns?\n\n1. $rank(A) = 3$\n2. $A$ is invertible\n3. $nullity(A) = 1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184757 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50dd3dbf-46fe-5a60-971b-ec20882fc4a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184937 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186185007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184937 #edb/ref 1061 19992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184937 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af600efe-5480-5b61-8280-594a0825dbc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184937 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184938 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184938 #edb/ref 1061 19993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184938 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "526a000d-4f1e-513e-83a7-dcec4069077d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184938 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184939 #edb/ref 1061 19994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184939 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "10431142-f052-51d9-b9c7-38e48410c92d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184939 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185007 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185007 #edb/ref 1061 37511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185007 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3c8115af-900b-592a-a3df-6c126d87446c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185007 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185320 #edb/ref 1064 "When we reduce a matrix to row echelon form, the number of *pivot* columns plus the number of *non-pivot* columns always equals the total number of columns of the matrix. \n\nBy translating this into the language of vector spaces, we get the **rank-nullity theorem**. The rank-nullity theorem relates the rank of a matrix to the dimension of its null space:\n\n*Let $A$ be a matrix (not necessarily square) with $n$ columns. Then,*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Col}(A)) + \\textrm{dim(Null}(A)) = \\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n),\n$$\n\n*or, equivalently,*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that for some matrix $A,$ we have\n\n![](images/30/30f6472f02a4ac47f285fff05e0cdbf56367acdd18231271dc3dccf0040901bd.svg)\n\nWe can use the rank-nullity theorem to calculate $\\textrm{rank}(A).$\n\nIn this case, we must have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, we must have $\\textrm{nullity}(A)=2,$ since \n![](images/b5/b5f38590a71a2f8e1f29a5ba98920a10037737c1ebcbd3fd60f08a3e47555141.svg)\n\nTherefore, using the rank-nullity theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185320 #edb/ref 1065 3524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185320 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0728c510-110b-5bde-93ea-9050a87a6ecc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185320 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269912 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269912 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269912 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4635be5a-a6fe-56a4-8e10-c0910f4f8147" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269938 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269938 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269938 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "49f0ab4c-fbd4-51c3-9786-ba43e794d43c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270041 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270041 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270041 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "576e17c2-77cc-5c9e-8c7f-5cabff8304b1" #edb/ref 17592