1068 #edb/ref 17592186271871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272246 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272246 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272246 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A$ be a $5\\times 7$ matrix. If $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3},{b}_{4}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$, find $\\text{dim(Null}(A)).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272246 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a8591660-5b8e-5e5d-b7c5-293cb529794e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272246 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=7,$ and since $B$ is formed by $4$ vectors, $\\text{rank}(A)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =7 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =7-4 \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348222 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452721 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186348222 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452722 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186348222 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452723 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186348222 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79420" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348222 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348222 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix with only  $1$ pivot column?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- **II.** $A$ is not  invertible\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A)=3$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186348222 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "251cb9e2-c851-5082-b166-e39206cbabf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348222 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ has just $1$ pivot column, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the matrix $A,$ the number of the columns is $n=4.$ Now, since $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem,  $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. According to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 1+\\text{nullity}(A) & =4 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375944 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454443 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186375944 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454444 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186375944 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454445 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186375944 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139986" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375944 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375944 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}?\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The number of non-pivot columns in $A$ is equal to $3$\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4}$\n- **III.** $\\text{rank}(A)=3$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186375944 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8048be79-9af8-51c0-a0c1-5b68f549139c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375944 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since the generating vectors in \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\nare linearly independent, we conclude that the number of non-pivot columns in $A$ is equal to $1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=1\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=4.$ Now, according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+1 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186375972 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454452 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186375972 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454453 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186375972 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454454 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186375972 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375972 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375972 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}?\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5}$\n- **III.** $A$ is  invertible" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186375972 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "017db163-2592-561e-a975-d762dc265a45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375972 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=2.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=5,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =5 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =5-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{nullity}(A)=2\\ne 0,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{5}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=3\\ne 5$, according to the  invertible matrix theorem $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186377756 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186377757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377756 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79352" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377756 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377756 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186377756 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0131f756-450c-5ffd-bd43-d0c24b54342a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377756 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\text{nullity}(A)=3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186377757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377757 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186377762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377757 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377757 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377757 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186377760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377757 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0ef6c019-6203-53f2-a536-cc2b8d76a9fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377758 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377758 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377758 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aca86402-c665-55ad-96e2-e7faee870cc9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377759 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377759 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377759 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "701ec281-64bf-55a9-a13a-9af209eaff5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377760 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377760 